duce the risk of  spreading the virus  they don't eliminate it in the setting  of covid19  health information is beginning to be  used  in a transactional sense much more than  before  chester drum is a doctor at the national  university hospital in singapore  and co-founded aok pass he expects that  in order for people to return to  pre-pandemic life  they'll have to show proof of their  health status let me show you part of my  medical record to get on an airplane  let me show you that i've had a  vaccination procedure in order to go  to an event for example this has also  meant forgeries  for instance last november seven people  in paris were arrested and charged with  selling fake coven 19 test results  that cost as much as 360 dollars  drum says the app fights counterfeiting  using blockchain technology  so that the test result and vaccination  certificates are unique and linked to  only one individual  the remarkable thing about blockchain is  once it's entered you can't change it  while blockchain-backed apps may prevent  fakes there could be data leaks  they pose a variety of essential  questions  for the protection of data privacy and  human rights  anna badewski is a researcher who  studies the ethical implication of  digital health data  police makers should require digital  health passport providers to  adopt appropriate technical and  organizational measures  to address potential data privacy  invasive situations  proactively public health researchers  have found that some people in india and  indonesia who tested positive and had  their results disclosed  were verbally attacked or socially  excluded in the u.s the centers for  disease control and prevention  recommends that community leaders and  public health officials  maintain the confidentiality of test  results to avoid social stigma  aokpas says it encrypts all personal  health information  and tries to keep to a minimum the data  it collects on its users  the company also said it does not  monetize data but has signed contracts  with airports and airlines to develop  and test the system  after nearly 10 months of testing aok  pass is scheduled to launch in all major  italian and french airports by the end  of february  it will have to compete with other  companies developing a similar tool  united and jetblue are preparing to use  an app developed by the world economic  forum  called the common pass while ibm is  creating a digital health pass using  blockchain  drum says figuring out the technical  parts of the digital health passport  were relatively easy  compared with making sure the app would  be used along each step of the travel  process  what's hard is the coordinated process  starting with a laboratory in a  different country creating a foreign  medical record  boarding an airline another highly  regulated industry  and then crossing an immigration gantry  which itself  is regulated by the rules and  regulations  of a country different than where you  got your your health test  when castaneda arrived in new york for a  layover on his way to dallas  he says airline staff didn't ask to see  proof of his health status  the rome airport authority said they're  educating travelers about the app  and plan to reach out to airlines  despite the patchy use of the app  castanada says the digital health  passport is a game changer  last time i traveled to the states was  march of 2020 right before the pandemic  hit so it's i mean it's nice to travel  [Music]  again
 

